WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.680
John: Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.110
It's actually Saturday but we're on our way
to Europe!

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.279
We've packed up and we're going to Europe,
but where are we going first, Henry?

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:08.860
Henry: North Carolina boo boo.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.630
John: North Carolina boo boo!

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.570
Alice, the sun is shining and the sky is blue
and we're feeling...?

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:15.720
Alice: Alwight!

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.859
John: Alright!

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:21.610
And now indeed, Hank, we are in Nashville
where noted American heroes Mom and Dad will

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.679
be taking care of our kids for the next week
while we're in Europe.

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:28.980
Mom and Dad's house has flowers and chickens
and goats and grilled cheese with the crust

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.980
cut off.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:30.980
Plus there is a fairy garden.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.320
All around the garden, Mom has installed all
of these tiny little fairies that everyone

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.320
can search for.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.620
It's like a scavenger hunt!

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.800
And there's a golden doodle?

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.430
I think she's currently urinating.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.430
Good job, Cassie!

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.790
Way to do that outside- oh it's poop, it's
a poop.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.070
And now having dropped off the kids we return
to the airport to fly to a somewhat less wooded

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.070
location.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.489
I have arrived in New York just in time to
get on the flight to Madrid, Hank.

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:56.850
So hopefully I will sleep for that entire
flight!

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:57.850
Nat: Hello!

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.719
John: Look who I found!

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:04.670
Here's an observation, videotaping yourself
with your eyes closed is not the same thing

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:05.670
as sleeping.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.300
I continue to find it very difficult to sleep
on airplanes, Hank.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.630
Because there is always a little voice inside
of me reminding me that I am 34,000 feet above

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:16.220
the ground which is a situation that calls
not for sleeping, but for ceaseless vigilance!

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.220
Nat: Uh...

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.259
I slept probably for an hour.

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:19.259
How you doing?

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:20.259
John: Yeah, not great.

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:22.060
So Hank, there are a lot of ways to deal with
jet lag.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.280
I don't like to judge people's ways.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.619
Our way today is that we're going to attempt
the Dory from Finding Nemo, which goes Just

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:31.619
Keep Swimming!

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:34.780
Took a shower, had some caffeine, changed
clothes, still haven't slept, feeling good,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.900
let's go to a museum!

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.409
So Hank, we're now at the Reina Sofia Art
Museum.

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:39.509
Just keep swimming!

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:42.950
Saw some cool stuff by Carl Andre, took one
of those glass elevators I hate while Nat

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:47.119
struggled to remain standing, saw a very good
early Picasso and then looked at people looking

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:48.820
at the great Picasso painting Guernica.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.900
And there was also this great Warhol and in
every room they had film from similar periods

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.770
of history, which was a fascinating curatorial
choice.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.970
Nat spent a long time watching this one.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.680
Nat and I have become really close over the
last year and a half and among the many gifts

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.110
he has given me is an appreciation for movies.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:09.000
I used to think of them as lesser novels,
but Nat has helped me to understand that movies

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.760
are valuable and fascinating of themselves.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.110
He's a great actor but he's also a brilliant
person and so let me quickly say while his

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.480
back is turned, I love you Nat Wolff.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.480
Alright moving on!

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.200
So now we're going to go to lunch!

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.129
It's, it's... fried bacon.

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:26.720
Okay Hank, Nat and I have to end the video
now because we're so tired and we still have

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.720
2 miles to walk, but...

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.410
Nat: I'm going to be John when I grow up.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.659
John: And I just hope that I'm Nat Wolff when
I grow up.

00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:34.140
Awww!

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.140
Hank I'll see you on Friday!

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.370
Nat: Ohh, I just got almost hit by a car.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:37.510
John: I'm glad you didn't get hit by that
car.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.230
That would have been really bad for press.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:39.400
Nat: [laughs]

